  "articleBody": "It might seem like a small detail, but how water flows off your roof can have a huge impact on your home's foundation. If your gutters are overflowing, downspouts are blocked, or water is simply dumping too close to your house, you could be setting yourself up for serious, expensive foundation issues. When water repeatedly saturates the soil around your foundation, it can lead to shifting, cracking, and structural damage over time. This isn't just a concern during heavy Texas storms; even consistent, light rain can cause problems if drainage isn't managed properly. You need to consider the complete path of water from your roof surface all the way to its final discharge point, not just the gutters themselves. Ignoring poor drainage now means risking potential leaks, mold, and costly repairs down the line.\n\n### Common Mistakes to Avoid\nMany homeowners overlook crucial aspects of roof drainage until it’s too late. One common mistake is installing gutters on fascia that's already deteriorated or unsupported, which means your new gutters won't do their job effectively. Another is using downspouts that are too small for the amount of roof area they need to drain, leading to overflow even with clean gutters. Relying on clogged gutter guards or screens without proper maintenance is also a frequent issue. Perhaps the biggest mistake is allowing downspouts to discharge right next to your foundation. Water should be directed at least five feet away, or into an underground system, to protect your home. Don’t forget to consider your site grading; sometimes, the land around your house actually directs water back towards the foundation even if your gutters are working well. Avoiding these common missteps is key to long-term foundation health.\n\nIf you're noticing recurring overflow, dampness around your foundation, or erosion near your home, it's time to get a professional opinion. Effective roof drainage protects your property's most critical structural elements. Discounted Roofing Co. offers comprehensive roof drainage assessments throughout the Dallas–Fort Worth region and surrounding areas. Contact us today to ensure your home is protected.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-03"
}